Hi all,

I'm a newbie with brms. I am trying to fit a measurement error model like this:

brm(formula = y  ~ 1 + me(x,xse) + (1 | subj)+(1 | item)

how do i specify a prior on x? I used get_prior to work out that I could define a prior on something called mexxse, which is a concatentation of the name in the measurement error part. Is this correct?

The model I want to fit in Stan syntax is:

model {
  vector[N] mu;
  //priors
  true_mvdur ~ normal(0, 200);
  meanvdur ~ normal(true_mvdur, se); // measurement model
  beta[1] ~ normal(0, 200);
  beta[2] ~ normal(0, 50);
  sigma_e ~ normal(0, 100);
  sigma_u ~ normal(0, 100);
  sigma_w ~ normal(0, 100);
  u ~ normal(0, sigma_u);
  w ~ normal(0, sigma_w);
  // likelihood
  mu = beta[1] + u[subj] + w[item] + beta[2] * true_mvdur[subj];
  y ~ normal(mu, sigma_e);
}

The formula I use is:

brm(formula = y  ~ 1 + me(meanvdur,se) + (1 | subj)+(1 | item)

Is this the correct translation from Stan to brms? Looking at the stancode() output from the brms model is not very easy to parse for me as there seem to be lots of internal variables.

The priors:

priors<-c(set_prior("normal(0,200)",
                    class = "b"),
                      set_prior("normal(0,50)", class = "b",coef="memeanvdurse"),
                      set_prior("normal(0,100)", class = "sd"),
          set_prior("normal(0,100)", class = "sigma")
          )

Hi Paul,

I am interested in reproducing the eight schools example with brms. More specifically, I am interested in extracting theta.

With the Eight Schools Data
schools_dat <- list(J = seq(1,8,1),
                    y = c(28,  8, -3,  7, -1,  1, 18, 12),
                    sigma = c(15, 10, 16, 11,  9, 11, 10, 18))

I'd think to run brm(y|se(sigma) ~ 1 + (1|J), data = schools_dat)
However, I am only able to recover eta and mu. How can I get posteriors of theta from brms output?

 see below for Stan Code from https://github.com/stan-dev/rstan/wiki/RStan-Getting-Started

// saved as 8schools.stan
data {
  int<lower=0> J; // number of schools
  real y[J]; // estimated treatment effects
  real<lower=0> sigma[J]; // s.e. of effect estimates
}
parameters {
  real mu;
  real<lower=0> tau;
  real eta[J];
}
transformed parameters {
  real theta[J];
  for (j in 1:J)
    theta[j] = mu + tau * eta[j];
}
model {
  target += normal_lpdf(eta | 0, 1);
  target += normal_lpdf(y | theta, sigma);
}

Go for coef(<brmsmodel>)$J to extract the summary estimates of theta and coef(<brmsmodel>, summary = FALSE)$J to extract the posterior samples themselves.

